{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790349523,"nonce":"mlyESYEl0/creC0zMS4XmYHxLaE=","title":"An AOT executable whose main returns nothing exits with an undefined status","message":"compile_llvm (crates/zyntax_cli/src/backends/llvm_aot.rs:103) makes the program's own `main` the C entry point. ZynML's `def main()` lowers to `define void @main()`, so the process exits with whatever the return register holds, for programs whose output is otherwise right. Any script or CI step that runs the binary sees a failure. A `def main(): i64` exits with its value truncated to 8 bits, which is at least defined.\n\nReproducer:\n\n    extern def putchar(c: i32): i32\n    def main() {\n        putchar(65)\n        putchar(10)\n    }\n\n`zyntax compile --backend llvm -g crates/zynml/ml.zyn -s p.zynml -o p \u0026\u0026 ./p; echo $?` prints A and exits 10 on Mac arm64 (putchar's return value, still in x0) and 232 on the NUC (x86_64).\n\nFix: emit a C `int main(int, char**)` that calls the program's entry and returns 0 for a void entry, and keep the entry under its own name. The ignored case a_main_returning_nothing_exits_zero in crates/zyntax_cli/tests/llvm_aot_zynml.rs (branch aot-reval) covers it.","files":null}]}